("OralIllusions", "Spatial Disorientation", "Aeromedical",
 "The vestibular illusions come from the inner ear losing the plot; the visual ones come from the eye being fooled on approach. The gradeable half is not the name — it is what each one makes you DO. Nearly every approach illusion drives you LOW; the exceptions are a wide runway, downsloping terrain, fog and bright approach lighting.",
 ["The leans ➔ a bank entered too slowly to be sensed, then corrected abruptly. Makes you roll back into the original bank",
  "Coriolis ➔ an abrupt head movement in a prolonged constant-rate turn. Makes you manoeuvre violently to stop a rotation that is not happening",
  "Graveyard spiral ➔ losing altitude in a long coordinated turn. Makes you pull back, tightening the spiral",
  "Somatogravic ➔ rapid acceleration feels like a pitch up. Makes you push into a dive. Deceleration does the reverse",
  "Inversion ➔ abrupt level-off from a climb feels like tumbling backwards. Makes you push abruptly nose low",
  "Elevator ➔ an updraft feels like a pitch up. Makes you push nose low. A downdraft does the reverse",
  "False horizon ➔ sloping cloud, an obscured horizon, ground lights mistaken for stars. Puts you in a dangerous attitude",
  "Autokinesis ➔ stare at a single light in the dark and it appears to move. Makes you chase it",
  "Narrow runway ➔ you feel HIGH, so you fly LOW and risk landing short. A wide runway does the reverse",
  "Upsloping runway or terrain ➔ you feel HIGH, so you fly LOW. Downsloping does the reverse",
  "Black hole or featureless terrain ➔ water, snow or darkness with no cues. You feel high and fly LOW",
  "Rain on the windscreen ➔ refraction makes you feel high, so you fly LOW. Haze does the same",
  "Fog ➔ the odd one out — penetrating fog feels like a pitch up, so you steepen the approach abruptly",
  "Bright runway and approach lighting ➔ makes the runway feel CLOSER, so you fly HIGH. Dim lighting does the reverse"]),

